From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 23:02:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(control): report mouse input to the application (GD-07)

SendMouseEvent returned false and IsVtMouseModeEnabled returned false, so
Windows Terminal never routed pointer input down its VT path for a
ghostty pane. Applications that asked for the mouse still saw events -
the selection path drives ghostty's mouse and ghostty reports what it
receives - but they were reading the selection machinery rather than
their input:

  - presses arrived twice: SetSelectionAnchor presses, and
    LeftClickOnTerminal replays one press per click of a multi-click,
    against a single release site
  - a plain single click sent no press at all; that path only clears the
    selection, deliberately, because pressing on WT's rounded click
    position latched ghostty's anchor a character late
  - right, middle and the wheel never arrived, because the selection path
    only ever passes GHOSTTY_MOUSE_LEFT

Reported from use with a raw crossterm probe: one physical click produced
two Down and one Up, sharing a timestamp to the millisecond, and zero
BUTTON2 events across a long session. The report's leading hypothesis was
that we answer in both urxvt (1015) and SGR (1006) encodings; that is not
it - the duplication happens before encoding.

IsVtMouseModeEnabled now answers from ghostty_surface_mouse_captured,
which is the same question ghostty asks itself when deciding whether to
report, so the two sides cannot disagree about the mode. SendMouseEvent
moves the pointer with modifiers, then dispatches press/release for left,
right and middle, and scroll on both axes.

Scroll is divided by WHEEL_DELTA and not negated: ghostty documents
"negative is down, left; positive is up, right" and non-precision offsets
are wheel ticks, which is Windows' convention on both signs.

No arbitration code here. ControlInteractivity::_canSendVTMouseInput
already chooses this path over selection and already implements holding
shift to suppress reporting so a user can always select - shared with
cascadia, so both engines obey one rule.

Confirmed working by the reporter against the probe that found it.
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 126 ++++++++++++++++--
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h      |   2 +-
 2 files changed, 116 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index 0718be156..88e1ccc85 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -859,16 +859,109 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         }
     }
 
-    bool GhosttyControlCore::SendMouseEvent(const Core::Point /*viewportPosCore*/,
-                                            const uint32_t /*uiButton*/,
-                                            const ControlKeyStates /*states*/,
-                                            const int16_t /*wheelDelta*/,
+    // Mouse reporting: the path an application takes when it has asked for the
+    // mouse with DECSET 1000/1002/1003/1006 and friends.
+    //
+    // Returning false here used to mean "not handled", which routed every
+    // pointer event down WT's *selection* path instead. Applications still saw
+    // events, because the selection path drives ghostty's mouse to make
+    // selections and ghostty reports whatever mouse input it receives - but
+    // what they saw was the shape of the selection machinery rather than their
+    // input: presses arrived twice (SetSelectionAnchor presses, and
+    // LeftClickOnTerminal replays one press per click of a multi-click), a
+    // plain single click sent no press at all, and the right button, the
+    // middle button and the wheel never arrived because the selection path only
+    // ever sends GHOSTTY_MOUSE_LEFT. See GD-07 and docs/MOUSE-DUPLICATE-REPORTS.md.
+    //
+    // The arbitration between "this is a selection" and "the application owns
+    // the mouse" is not ours: ControlInteractivity::_canSendVTMouseInput picks
+    // this path over the selection path, and it also implements the convention
+    // that holding shift suppresses reporting so a user can always select. All
+    // this has to do is answer honestly and do what it is told.
+    bool GhosttyControlCore::SendMouseEvent(const Core::Point viewportPosCore,
+                                            const uint32_t uiButton,
+                                            const ControlKeyStates states,
+                                            const int16_t wheelDelta,
                                             const Control::MouseButtonState /*state*/)
     {
-        // Mouse reporting is Phase 6 (selection and interaction parity). The
-        // pane is fully usable from the keyboard before then; returning false
-        // means "not handled", which is honest rather than silently swallowing.
-        return false;
+        if (!_surface || !ghostty_surface_mouse_captured(_surface))
+        {
+            return false;
+        }
+
+        auto mods = GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE;
+        if (states.IsShiftPressed()) { mods = static_cast<ghostty_input_mods_e>(mods | GHOSTTY_MODS_SHIFT); }
+        if (states.IsCtrlPressed()) { mods = static_cast<ghostty_input_mods_e>(mods | GHOSTTY_MODS_CTRL); }
+        if (states.IsAltPressed()) { mods = static_cast<ghostty_input_mods_e>(mods | GHOSTTY_MODS_ALT); }
+
+        // Every report carries a position, so the pointer moves first whatever
+        // the event is. ghostty derives the reported cell from this.
+        _mouseTo(viewportPosCore, mods);
+
+        const auto button = [&]() {
+            switch (uiButton)
+            {
+            case WM_LBUTTONDOWN:
+            case WM_LBUTTONUP:
+                return GHOSTTY_MOUSE_LEFT;
+            case WM_RBUTTONDOWN:
+            case WM_RBUTTONUP:
+                return GHOSTTY_MOUSE_RIGHT;
+            case WM_MBUTTONDOWN:
+            case WM_MBUTTONUP:
+                return GHOSTTY_MOUSE_MIDDLE;
+            default:
+                return GHOSTTY_MOUSE_UNKNOWN;
+            }
+        }();
+
+        switch (uiButton)
+        {
+        case WM_LBUTTONDOWN:
+        case WM_RBUTTONDOWN:
+        case WM_MBUTTONDOWN:
+            ghostty_surface_mouse_button(_surface, GHOSTTY_MOUSE_PRESS, button, mods);
+            // The selection path synthesises a release before it starts or ends
+            // a selection, keyed off this flag. A press sent from here must set
+            // it too, or leaving mouse mode mid-drag leaves ghostty believing a
+            // button is still down.
+            _mouseDown = true;
+            break;
+
+        case WM_LBUTTONUP:
+        case WM_RBUTTONUP:
+        case WM_MBUTTONUP:
+            ghostty_surface_mouse_button(_surface, GHOSTTY_MOUSE_RELEASE, button, mods);
+            _mouseDown = false;
+            break;
+
+        case WM_MOUSEWHEEL:
+        case WM_MOUSEHWHEEL:
+        {
+            // ghostty documents its own convention on scrollCallback: "negative
+            // is down, left; positive is up, right", and in a non-precision
+            // event the offset is in wheel *ticks*. Windows agrees on both
+            // signs, so this is a division by WHEEL_DELTA and not a negation -
+            // getting that wrong inverts scrolling, which is easy to ship and
+            // annoying to live with.
+            const auto ticks = static_cast<double>(wheelDelta) / WHEEL_DELTA;
+            const auto x = uiButton == WM_MOUSEHWHEEL ? ticks : 0.0;
+            const auto y = uiButton == WM_MOUSEHWHEEL ? 0.0 : ticks;
+            // Scroll mods are precision/momentum, which a Windows wheel has
+            // neither of; a mouse wheel is exactly the non-precision case.
+            ghostty_surface_mouse_scroll(_surface, x, y, 0);
+            break;
+        }
+
+        default:
+            // WM_MOUSEMOVE and anything else: the position above is the whole
+            // event. ghostty decides whether motion is worth reporting from the
+            // modes the application set (1002 wants drags, 1003 wants all
+            // motion), which is not a decision to duplicate here.
+            break;
+        }
+
+        return true;
     }
 
     // The scrollbar's thumb, dragged. WT gives an absolute buffer row for the
@@ -939,7 +1032,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
     // and for ghostty truncating to whole pixels. The cell *centre*, which this
     // used to send, also lands below 60% - the far end of a drag was never the
     // bug - but 0.1 of a cell is not a margin worth relying on.
-    void GhosttyControlCore::_mouseTo(const Core::Point cell) const
+    void GhosttyControlCore::_mouseTo(const Core::Point cell, const ghostty_input_mods_e mods) const
     {
         if (!_surface)
         {
@@ -963,7 +1056,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         const auto cellHeight = _cellSize.Height / _compositionScale;
         const auto x = (static_cast<double>(cell.X) + 0.25) * cellWidth;
         const auto y = (static_cast<double>(cell.Y) + 0.5) * cellHeight;
-        ghostty_surface_mouse_pos(_surface, x, y, GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE);
+        ghostty_surface_mouse_pos(_surface, x, y, mods);
     }
 
     // Windows Terminal never tells the core that the button came up: only VT
@@ -1424,7 +1517,18 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
     winrt::hstring GhosttyControlCore::HoveredUriText() const { return {}; }
     IReference<Core::Point> GhosttyControlCore::HoveredCell() const { return nullptr; }
 
-    bool GhosttyControlCore::IsVtMouseModeEnabled() const { return false; }
+    // Whether the application has asked for the mouse. This is the switch that
+    // makes ControlInteractivity route pointer events to SendMouseEvent instead
+    // of the selection path, so answering false - as this did - is what left
+    // applications reading the selection machinery's leftovers (GD-07).
+    //
+    // ghostty_surface_mouse_captured is the same question ghostty asks itself
+    // when deciding whether to report, so the two sides cannot disagree about
+    // which mode the terminal is in.
+    bool GhosttyControlCore::IsVtMouseModeEnabled() const
+    {
+        return _surface && ghostty_surface_mouse_captured(_surface);
+    }
     bool GhosttyControlCore::ShouldSendAlternateScroll(const uint32_t /*uiButton*/, const int32_t /*delta*/) const { return false; }
     // Where the cursor is, in cells, for the IME candidate window.
     //
diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
index e92454467..bce44549d 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
@@ -266,7 +266,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
 
         // Selection is driven through ghostty's mouse handling, because that
         // is the only thing that both sets the selection *and* draws it.
-        void _mouseTo(Core::Point cell) const;
+        void _mouseTo(Core::Point cell, ghostty_input_mods_e mods = GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE) const;
         void _releaseMouseIfHeld() const;
 
         // Output has to draw itself; libghostty's wakeup coalesces. Same
